\begin_layout Standard
Let the observed input be 
\begin_inset Formula $X=x$
\end_inset

.
 Then say 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{P}\left(h(x)=1\mid X=x\right)=\gamma_{x}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{P}\left(h(x)=0\mid X=x\right)=1-\gamma_{x}$
\end_inset

 then the expected loss is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(y,h(x))\mid X=x\right] & =\sum_{\hat{y}\in\{0,1\}}\sum_{y\in\{0,1\}}\mathbb{P}\left(Y=y,h(x)=\hat{y}\mid X=x\right)\ell(y,\hat{y})\\
 & =\sum_{\hat{y}\in\{0,1\}}\sum_{y\in\{0,1\}}\mathbb{P}\left(Y=y\mid X=x\right)\mathbb{P}\left(h(x)=\hat{y}\mid X=x\right)\ell(y,\hat{y})\\
\\ & =\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=x\right)\mathbb{P}\left(h(0)=1\mid X=x\right)\ell(1,1)\\
 & \,+\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=x\right)\mathbb{P}\left(h(0)=0\mid X=x\right)\ell(1,0)\\
 & \,+\mathbb{P}\left(Y=0\mid X=x\right)\mathbb{P}\left(h(0)=1\mid X=x\right)\ell(0,1)\\
 & \,+\mathbb{P}\left(Y=0\mid X=x\right)\mathbb{P}\left(h(0)=0\mid X=x\right)\ell(0,0)\\
\\ & =\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=x\right)\mathbb{P}\left(h(0)=0\mid X=x\right)\\
 & \,+\mathbb{P}\left(Y=0\mid X=x\right)\mathbb{P}\left(h(0)=1\mid X=x\right)\\
\\ & =\gamma_{x}\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=x\right)+(1-\gamma_{x})\mathbb{P}\left(Y=0\mid X=x\right),
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the first equal sign uses the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is independent of our hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
 Using the above we calculate the risk
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(y,h(x))\right] & =\mathbb{E}\left[\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(y,h(x))\mid X=x\right]\right]\\
 & =\sum_{x\in\{0,1\}}\mathbb{P}\left(X=x\right)\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(y,h(x))\mid X=x\right]\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}\left(\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(y,h(x))\mid X=0\right]\right)+\frac{1}{2}\left(\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(y,h(x))\mid X=1\right]\right)\\
\\ & =\frac{1}{2}\left(\gamma_{0}\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=0\right)+(1-\gamma_{0})\mathbb{P}\left(Y=0\mid X=0\right)\right)\\
 & \,+\frac{1}{2}\left(\gamma_{1}\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=1\right)+(1-\gamma_{1})\mathbb{P}\left(Y=0\mid X=1\right)\right)
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because this is linear in the probabilities the answer is immediately apparent.
 When minimizing w.r.t.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{0}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{1}$
\end_inset

 the optimal strategy will deterministically choose the output that is most likely given 
\series bold

\begin_inset Formula $X=x$
\end_inset


\series default
,
 in most cases.
 If the probabilities of 
\begin_inset Formula $Y=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y=1$
\end_inset

 are equal then the weights 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{x}$
\end_inset

 does not matter.
 Formally:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\gamma^{*}_{x}=\mathbb{P}\left(h(x)=1\mid X=x\right)=\begin{cases}
1 & \text{, if }\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=x\right)>\mathbb{P}\left(Y=0\mid X=x\right)\\
0 & \text{, if }\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=x\right)<\mathbb{P}\left(Y=0\mid X=x\right)\\{}
[0,1] & \text{, if }\mathbb{P}\left(Y=1\mid X=x\right)=\mathbb{P}\left(Y=0\mid X=x\right)
\end{cases}
\]

